tiers.question_style:
Monitor first: a lead II strip, with the 12-lead behind a button. tier_question: 'What does this ECG show?', with plain-language options plus the name (a new 'plain' field on diagnoses), for example 'Fast irregular rhythm, no P waves (atrial fibrillation)'.

Every core case then asks 'What do you do first?' with four fixed categories:
(A) start BLS/AED and call the arrest team;
(B) call the doctor now, keep the patient monitored with the defibrillator at hand, and record a 12-lead;
(C) check the electrodes and record again;
(D) document and report at the round.

A hint button is available. An optional 20-s triage mode unlocks at 80% accuracy or more.

A monitor-style II strip first, with the 12-lead on tap, and a one-line prehospital vignette ('Chest pain for 40 min, BP 85/50').

Then 'Next action' with four fixed paramedic categories:
(A) shock/CPR per ERC;
(B) pads on, pacing or synchronised cardioversion ready, request physician support;
(C) transmit the 12-lead and pre-alert a PCI centre;
(D) monitor, transport and hand over.

Once imported, arrest strips ask 'Shockable?', with a 10-s timer after 80% accuracy. A hint button is available. The tier carries a Preview label until the arrest strips ship.

risks_en:
Learners memorise 58 pictures instead of patterns, and the lessons alone show about 30 of them. Fixes:
- at least 3 tracings per core diagnosis per tier before a tier leaves preview;
- the first scored attempt at a lesson ECG does not count towards mastery;
- graduation and mastery use unseen tracings;
- each review rotates the question, the follow-up and the first view;
- the headline metric is accuracy on tracings seen for the first time.

Action tiers built as a lighter student tier, or reaching beyond scope. Without VF, VT, asystole and artefact the paramedic tier rehearses the wrong priorities, and drug decisions for nurses go beyond their scope in Romania. Fixes:
- the paramedic tier stays in preview until the arrest-strip set is reviewed;
- two fixed action-category sets;
- no drug doses anywhere;
- ERC as taught in Romania, worded 'per local protocol';
- one review by a UPU or SMURD emergency physician.

False certainty in the answer keys. The surface ECG cannot prove RV capture (site-13), the cause of a device change (trbl-06), undersensing vs asynchronous pacing (trbl-04) or pacemaker dependency (pace-08). The reference values are machine measurements that agree only to within about 20 ms. Fixes:
- 'Cannot be determined: interrogation needed' is a scored answer;
- follow-ups quote only approved findings and flag the items the writer must verify;
- drill bands are never tighter than the reference;
- a second reader checks every device case.

Review load: 58 cases × 6 tiers × RO/EN cannot all be written, checked and approved. Fixes:
- three explanation depths instead of six;
- action text at diagnosis level, with case overrides only where needed;
- two fixed action-category sets, approved once;
- tier_options only where the defaults do not fit;
- 58 follow-ups in total, each teaching something the main question does not.

Distractors that are never the answer: 16 keys appear only as distractors, so test-wise learners learn to eliminate them. Fixes:
- add real cases for those keys;
- once an item has about 100 answers per tier, run item analysis (difficulty, discrimination, distractors chosen by fewer than 5% of learners) and replace distractors that do not work.

The tier read as a measure of competence, or as a cage: an ATI specialist may be weaker on pacing than a cardiology resident. Fixes:
- the tier sets defaults only, and the Library shows everything;
- the placement check marks lessons done;
- switching keeps history;
- mastery is stored per tier;
- specialists have a focus setting.

Guideline drift, and the action box read as a protocol. CHA₂DS₂-VA (2024), STEMI equivalents (2023), ERC 2025, the 50 vs 60/min bradycardia cut-off and the conduction-system pacing consensus (2023) all change over time. Fixes:
- every action and deep text carries a guideline tag and a review date;
- review once a year after the ESC congress, and after each ERC release;
- keep the 'teaching material, not a protocol' note.
